# Web client

- The shared game certificate warning compares the installed certificate SANs to panel hosts, including one-label DNS wildcards and IP addresses.

- The only UI: React 19, TypeScript (strict), Vite, Tailwind v4, Radix dialogs, TanStack Query, TanStack Router (code-based route tree in `src/router.tsx`, routes code-split with `lazyRouteComponent`), TanStack Virtual for log windowing.
- Wiring: `src/main.tsx` builds `AppServices` (`src/app/services.ts`: API client, endpoints, `SessionStore`, `SessionRefresher`, QueryClient) and renders `AppRoot`, which reacts to session events. Tests build the same services over a fake `Transport`; never add module-level singletons.
- API layer (`src/api`): `client.ts` owns `fetch`/`XMLHttpRequest` for panel API calls; every failure is an `ApiError` (`errors.ts`) parsed from the error envelope or mapped to `network_error`/`invalid_response`/`aborted`. Components show `describeError(err)` and the request id, never response text. All wire types come from `types.gen.ts` (generated; run `scripts/gen-types.sh`). Add routes to `endpoints.ts`, query keys to `query-keys.ts`, hooks to `queries.ts`.
- Session: token in memory, mirrored to sessionStorage (default) or localStorage ("Keep me signed in"), key `golem_deploy_session`; tabs sync over the `golem_deploy_session` BroadcastChannel. A 401 `unauthenticated` for the current token ends the session once (`SessionStore.handleUnauthenticated`). `SessionRefresher` refreshes 30 minutes before the JWT `exp` and on tab return after long inactivity. The displayed role comes from `GET /account`, never from the JWT.
- Credentials only as `Authorization: Bearer`; never cookies. Streams (job logs, server logs) use `client.stream` + `readLines` (`src/api/stream.ts`) through `useLogStream`; the contract has no resume offset, so streams are not auto-reconnected.
- React Query: no retry on 4xx, polling only for state that changes on its own or through other users and only while visible: job, project, server and project-build lists use `listPollInterval` (2 s while something is in progress, 10 s otherwise), plus job and build details, server detail, stats, the runtime view (only while `state` is `running`, no retries) and status. A finished job invalidates builds too (a start records the schema fingerprint). Per-row actions own their mutation so pending state stays per row. Every job-returning action shows the job inline (`InlineJob`).
- UI primitives live in `src/components/ui`; reuse them instead of new class strings. Render server strings as text; no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`; links from server data go through `safeHttpUrl`.
- The SPA's CSP allows `connect-src 'self'` plus HTTPS and WSS to the effective game host. Keep the build free of inline scripts, eval and remote assets. Only the browser connection test (`src/lib/connection-test.ts`) opens direct game connections, with no API credentials or game messages. It reads `/connectivity` and `/runtime` on demand and closes all probes on success, failure, timeout and navigation. A browser without WebTransport can still run HTTPS and the WebSocket fallback. After a successful WebSocket probe, report UDP reachability and an unserved WebTransport path as possible causes of WebTransport failure.
- Settings uses separate game, panel and ACME certificate queries and refreshes them while visible to pick up renewals. Keep the reported mode while a restricted issue job runs and show its status, detail and log through `InlineJob`. Terminal certificate jobs refresh the certificate and server queries. A changed panel certificate prompts a reload. Warn about the mounted key when a shared game certificate and the panel use the game host.
- Contract mirrors: client validators (`validateOrigin` in `src/components/origins-editor.tsx`, `validateAddonTarget` in `src/components/static-addons.tsx`, `validateGameHost` in `src/pages/admin-settings.tsx`, `checkServerImport` for export format 2 in `src/lib/server-import.ts`, `src/lib/validators.ts`: repo URLs with the daemon's wording and `sameRepoOrigin` (an origin change requires new auth), env keys (only `GOLEM_` is reserved) with case-insensitive uniqueness, env values (single line, 32 KiB per variable counted in bytes, 128 variables / 64 KiB per set), static names, ports; `authTypesFor` offers no "none" for SSH URLs) and `jobPermissions` (`src/components/jobs.tsx`: restricted jobs, cancel by creator or admin) copy daemon rules for early feedback only; still handle the daemon's 4xx where the action happens. Change them together with the Go rule they mirror.
- Errors: `describeError` explains codes the daemon cannot (`bad_host`, `invalid_credentials`, ...) and otherwise shows the envelope message; `withWait` merges `Retry-After` into it as one sentence (a trailing "try again later" is replaced, not repeated). A 429 on opening a log stream is shown inside the `LogViewer` (`notice`) with a retry. Field errors go where the field is: `auth.*`/`auth` in project dialogs, `env.<KEY>`/`env_set.<KEY>` to the env editor row (`envErrorsFrom`), `allowed_origins[i]` to the origin row (`originErrorsFrom`), `static_addons[i].name|target` to the add-on row, `build_id` on the build select, `game_host`, `certificate_pem`/`private_key_pem` on their fields, `servers[i].*` named in the import dialog. `job_in_progress` and every other new failure (build details, start failures, 409 reasons of build/project/image/static deletes) are shown as the daemon worded them, never replaced by generic text. Job and build details are plain text and wrap in full.
- Builds: the project page's Builds section starts builds (`Pull first` is forced while there is no checkout, otherwise off) and lists them newest first; a build is a `project.build` job of the project, shown with `InlineJob`; `/builds/$id` shows details and the build job's log. Builds carry only `created_by` (an id): the username comes from the project's job list. Admins delete builds; static add-ons and `allow_custom_dockerfile` are admin edits on the project page.
- Servers are created from `GET /builds?status=success` (project select, newest build preselected; `/servers?create=<project id>` opens the dialog for a project); with none, explain the project, build, server order instead of showing a form. `GettingStarted` shows that order while no server exists. A server's build changes only through the deploy dialog (other successful builds of its project; any build for a server without one); for a running server it offers PATCH then `POST /restart` and says players are disconnected. `Server.certificate` and `GameCertificate.certificate` may be null in every mode: show both cases. The private key of a certificate upload lives only in the form and is cleared on success. Actions follow `state` (`status-badges.tsx`: `portLocked`, `serverActionsBlocked`): the port is locked while the container runs/restarts/is paused or the state is `unknown` (Docker unreachable for a server with a container), `unknown` disables start/stop/restart/delete and log reads, `missing` (no container) offers start but no restart or logs. `DELETE /servers/{id}` waits for its `server.delete` job: success is a toast, a failure is an error toast plus the job shown inline. Server logs arrive sanitised: the viewer dims escaped sequences (`\x1b[31m`, `\u202e`) and the job log's `container | ` prefix (`logSegments`) and never interprets them.
- Static files: the page shows `used_bytes`/`quota_bytes` (0: unlimited)/`file_count`/`max_files` and warns when queued files cannot fit (`fitWarning`). One file per request, so each row's status is its request's all-or-nothing outcome (5xx and network errors: "outcome unknown"); `createLimiter` in `client.ts` caps upload requests of the whole tab at `MAX_CONCURRENT_UPLOADS` (2, the daemon's per-account cap) and the page waits out a 429 from uploads elsewhere. `replace=1` only for files the user confirmed; a 409 offers Replace on that row; a 507 marks the file not stored.
- Mock layer: `src/mock/transport.ts` stands in for the Docker-, git- and file-backed routes (projects, builds, servers and their runtime and connectivity metadata, images, static, volumes, certificate settings and jobs; the game host is the page's host name) under `VITE_API_MOCK=1 npm run dev` (`GOLEM_DEPLOY_DEV_API` overrides the dev proxy target), following the final contract (access levels via the real `GET /account`, restricted jobs, follow cap, tail bounds, replace semantics, first-error import validation of format 2, SSH auth and origin rules, env field names, port lock, `job_in_progress` for port and build changes, same-project builds, build lifecycle and delete rules, runtime 409/503, certificate modes and field errors, add-on rules, `unknown` state, static usage/quota/upload cap, escaped log output). Its seed includes failure cases (a failing build, a crashing start, a silent admin endpoint, a server without a build). `src/mock/transport.test.ts` pins those rules. Keep it in step with contract changes or delete the domain from it; it must never behave differently from the daemon. It is imported only behind `import.meta.env.DEV`; `vite.config.ts` refuses to build with the flag and fails if the mock reaches the bundle.
- Verification: `npm run lint` (zero warnings), `npm test` (Vitest + Testing Library, jsdom; `src/test/contract.test.tsx`, `src/test/contract-updates.test.tsx` and `src/test/milestone3.test.tsx` pin contract behaviour; wire fixtures with every field are in `src/test/fixtures.ts`), `npm run build` (type-check + bundle for Go embedding). For UI changes that touch the daemon, also run the built binary against a temp data dir and exercise the flow in a browser (a project, a build, a server started from it, its runtime view and game URL).
